{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"Research England's total capital funding for UK universities falls about 29% in 2026-27, from £253m to £179m, because one-off capital lines end.","detail":"Table 4 of Research England letter RE-CL-2026-03 shows total capital funding of £253m in 2025-26 and £179m in 2026-27. The £59m 'additional capital formula funding' and the £15m UKRPIF line both go to n/a. 179/253 - 1 = -29.2%. The RCIF core stays at £174m in both years. UK university equipment budgets, including for sequencers, therefore have no one-off top-up from 2026-27.","url":"https://www.ukri.org/publications/research-england-funding-budgets-for-2026-to-2027/research-england-funding-budgets-2026-to-2027/research-england-funding-budgets-2026-to-2027/","date":"2026-03 (page read Oct 2026)","source_name":"UKRI / Research England funding budgets 2026-27, Table 4","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known file has only UK purchases and funding anecdotes (F437, F076). It has no quantified UK capital-budget series."},
{"label":"2B","direction":"supports","claim":"The UK RCIF is flat in cash at £174m and is restricted to sustaining existing facilities, so it cannot fund major new equipment.","detail":"The 2026-27 split is HEI Research Capital £90m (from £92m) and HERC £85m (from £83m), both rounded. The letter says RCIF 'must be directed toward sustaining existing research facilities' and 'should not be used for new buildings or major new infrastructure'. 2026-27 grants must be spent by March 2027. Flat cash is a real-terms cut, and the money can go to upkeep and equipment but not to new platforms. This limits new-sequencer purchases by UK research cores, which are demand for Illumina consumables and placements.","url":"https://www.ukri.org/publications/research-england-funding-budgets-for-2026-to-2027/research-england-funding-budgets-2026-to-2027/research-england-funding-budgets-2026-to-2027/","date":"2026-03","source_name":"UKRI / Research England, RE-CL-2026-03","source_type":"filing","quote":"must be directed toward sustaining existing research facilities","strength":"medium","why_new":"F437 states the narrowing qualitatively. This adds the by-line figures and the March 2027 spend deadline."},
{"label":"2B","direction":"supports","claim":"A further £50m RCIF cut is scheduled by 2029-30, about 29% of the £174m base, with reform starting in 2027-28.","detail":"Wonkhe (10 Mar 2026) reports a £50m cut by 2029-30 and a further reduction from 2027-28. It also quotes UKRI's December explainer: 'After tapering in financial year 2026 to 2027, investment in RCIF will be reformed'. My arithmetic: 174 - 50 = £124m, which is -28.7%. This assumes the £50m is measured against £174m. The Wonkhe piece is secondary, and the Research England letter gives no taper schedule.","url":"https://wonkhe.com/wonk-corner/research-capital-investment-fund-cuts-ahead/","date":"2026-03-10","source_name":"Wonkhe","source_type":"press","quote":"its use focused on sustaining current facilities","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known file has no UK RCIF taper figure. It only says UK equipment capital is narrowed (F437)."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"UKRI curiosity-driven research is flat to down in cash through 2029-30, which means a real-terms cut for UK academic research customers.","detail":"The UKRI budget explainer shows curiosity-driven research at £3,653m (2026-27), £3,581m, £3,612m and £3,638m (2029-30). 2027-28 is -2.0% nominal and 2029-30 is -0.4% vs 2026-27. Quality-related (QR) funding goes £2,258m to £2,161m (-4.3%) in 2027-28. Applicant-led grants go £815m to £821m (+0.7%). Inflation at roughly 2-3% a year (my assumption) would make these real declines. The UKRI total still rises, from £9,220m to £9,986m, but the growth goes to strategic and industry buckets.","url":"https://www.ukri.org/publications/explainer-ukri-budget-allocations/budget-allocations-for-uk-research-and-innovation/","date":"2026 (explainer)","source_name":"UKRI budget allocations explainer, Tables 1 and 3","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known file covers NIH and US funding only. This is a UK funding time series by line."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"MRC and BBSRC applicant-led budgets are about £113m and £112m a year and flat through 2029-30, well below earlier baselines.","detail":"The UKRI explainer's Table 4 shows MRC at 113/113/111/115 and BBSRC at 112/113/111/117 (£m, 2026-27 to 2029-30). THE (via search) quotes MRC discovery research at 'close to £200m' per year in the 2022-25 plan, and BBSRC investigator-driven projects at 'over £150m'. My arithmetic is -43% (113 vs 200) for MRC and -25% (112 vs 150) for BBSRC, but the baselines are approximate and the lines may not be like-for-like. These are the two councils that fund most UK genomics-heavy biology and biomedicine, and the plan has them paused for grant applications. This is a UK-wide signal of tight funding, not sequencing-specific.","url":"https://www.timeshighereducation.com/node/741567","date":"2026","source_name":"Times Higher Education / UKRI budget explainer","source_type":"press","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"The known file has no UK council-level budget numbers."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"35.6% of surveyed English universities are reducing support for research facilities and equipment.","detail":"A Dec 2025 survey by the Innovation and Research Caucus covered finance leaders at 74 English universities. It found 35.6% cutting support for research facilities and equipment and 36.8% scaling back internal research consortia. The Academy of Social Sciences summary (Feb 2026) cites it alongside a £6.2bn loss on UK research grants in 2023-24. I did not see the IRC report itself, so the sample details are unverified. It is directionally consistent with F076 and F437.","url":"https://acss.org.uk/policy-update-february-2026/","date":"2026-02","source_name":"Academy of Social Sciences policy update (citing IRC survey)","source_type":"other","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"The known file has no university-finance survey on equipment support."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"against","claim":"UKRI infrastructure spending and the total budget rise through 2029-30, so UK research capital is not collapsing.","detail":"The UKRI 'infrastructure' line is £486m, £502m, £485m, then £596m in 2029-30 (+22.6% vs 2026-27). The total budget rises from £9,220m to £9,986m (+8.3%). The RCIF core stays at £174m in 2026-27. The Delivery Plan page names UK Biobank proteomics on all 500,000 participants and Our Future Health at 3.75m participants as funded items. Neither carries a budget figure here, and neither is a clear Illumina volume.","url":"https://www.ukri.org/publications/explainer-ukri-budget-allocations/budget-allocations-for-uk-research-and-innovation/","date":"2026","source_name":"UKRI budget allocations explainer, Table 9","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"It is a watch-out: the UK public budget is growing overall and the cuts are concentrated in curiosity-driven and capital lines."}
